    "title": "Artemis III launch date: When NASA will land on the moon again",
    "text": "CAPE CANAVERAL, FLORIDA - MARCH 31: NASA's 322-foot-tall Artemis II Space Launch System rocket and Orion spacecraft stand on Launch Complex 39B at Kennedy Space Center on March 31, 2026 in Cape Canaveral, Florida. The 10-day mission will take NASA astronauts Commander Reid Wiseman, Pilot Victor Glover and Mission Specialist Christina Koch and CSA (Canadian Space Agency) Mission Specialist Jeremy Hansen around the moon and back. The astronauts are supposed to fly 230,000 miles out into space, the farthest any human has ever traveled from Earth. (Photo by Chip Somodevilla/Getty Images)Chip Somodevilla/Getty ImagesNASA’s Artemis II mission is set to launch Wednesday, but the mission’s biggest impact may be what comes next: Artemis III and a planned return to the moon.Artemis II will send four astronauts on a roughly 10-day flight around the moon — the first crewed lunar flyby since Apollo 17 in 1972 — to test the Orion spacecraft and other systems needed for future missions.Article continues below this adGet Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.ONLY 25¢Those systems are critical for Artemis III, the mission NASA is targeting to land astronauts on the moon for the first time in more than 50 years.The Artemis II crew includes Commander Reid Wiseman, Pilot Victor Glover, Mission Specialist Christina Koch and Canadian Space Agency astronaut Jeremy Hansen. The mission will travel about 230,000 miles from Earth, farther than any human has gone before.NASA officials say Artemis II will help determine whether the agency can stay on schedule for Artemis III.What to know about Artemis IIIWhen will Artemis III launch?NASA is targeting a 2027 launch for Artemis III, though that timeline depends on the success of Artemis II and continued testing of key systems.Article continues below this adWhat will Artemis III do?Artemis III is expected to attempt a crewed lunar landing — something NASA has not done since Apollo 17 in 1972.The mission will build on Artemis II by using tested life-support, navigation and communication systems needed to safely land astronauts on the moon and return them to Earth.Article continues below this adWhy does Artemis II matter for Artemis III?Artemis II is a test mission. It will evaluate the Orion spacecraft, deep-space navigation and crew safety systems.NASA officials say the data collected will determine whether Artemis III can move forward on schedule.“If Artemis II achieves its goals,” said Kevin Coggins, deputy associate administrator for NASA’s Space Communications and Navigation program, Artemis III will remain on track.Article continues below this adWhat happens after Artemis III?NASA plans additional lunar missions, including more landings as early as 2028, as part of a broader effort to establish a sustained human presence on the moon and prepare for future missions to Mars.What time is the Artemis II launch?Artemis II is scheduled to launch at 5:24 p.m. CT on Wednesday, April 1, 2026, from Launch Complex 39B at Kennedy Space Center in Florida.Article continues below this adGet alerted to the most important stories and stay on top of the news.April 1, 2026Marley Malenfant is a 10-year multimedia journalist who’s worked in print, digital, and broadcast. His prior work includes working in West Texas as a TV reporter for KTXS. During his time there, he covered education, sports, features, and breaking News. He’s skilled in front of the camera as well as behind it. He was a freelance writer, published in D Magazine, Dallas Morning News, SB Nation, Dallas Observer, and was a personality for “The Coach Brothers” sports radio show. Outside of journalism, he was an educator in the Dallas area. He was a 2012 Mayborn Literary Nonfiction Conference grant recipient. In 2021, he was part of the NABJ student multimedia project. He graduated with a paralegal degree from El Centro in 2023. Currently, he’s a trending news reporter for the Austin American-Statesman, covering breaking News, sports, music, and legal trends.",
